      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(slack): support per-channel replyToMode",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nSlack reply threading could only be configured for an account or chat type. Operators with channels that need different behavior could not keep one channel's replies at the parent level while threading replies elsewhere.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- Add `channels.slack.channels.<id>.replyToMode` to the existing per-channel Slack control surface.\n- Resolve the channel override before the account and chat-type fallback.\n- Carry that prepared mode through automatic replies, queued follow-ups, and same-channel message-tool sends/uploads so every path uses one decision.\n- Keep explicit inbound Slack thread targets authoritative.\n- Align config types, schema, generated metadata, tests, and Slack documentation.\n\n## User Impact\n\nSlack channel and private-channel entries can now set `replyToMode` to `off`, `first`, `all`, or `batched`. Automatic replies and same-channel `send` or `upload-file` tool calls honor the same effective override; channels without an override retain the existing account/chat-type behavior.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Focused Vitest: Slack config, prepare, threading-tool-context, action-runtime, and core reply plumbing — 253 tests passed.\n- Queued follow-up regression shard — 92 tests passed.\n- Broad Testbox changed gate: `tbx_01kwr3e4pea0ahhr69z59b857p` on Blacksmith, exit 0: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/28727583419\n- Live Slack proof: created a temporary parent message, posted a reply with an explicit parent timestamp, read the thread back to confirm Slack's `thread_ts`, then deleted both messages successfully.\n- Dependency contract inspected in installed `@slack/web-api`: file uploads forward `thread_ts`; Slack's `chat.postMessage` contract uses `thread_ts` for replies.\n- Fresh Codex autoreview on the complete branch: no accepted/actionable findings.\n",
